  "Question": "The equation \\(3y^2+y+4=2(6x^2+y+2)\\) where \\(y=2x\\) is satisfied by:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "no value of \\(x\\)",
    "B": "all values of \\(x\\)",
    "C": "\\(x=0\\) only",
    "D": "all integral values of \\(x\\) only",
    "E": "all rational values of \\(x\\) only"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Substitute \\(y=2x\\) so that the equation involves only \\(x\\):\n\\[\n3(2x)^2+2x+4=2(6x^2+2x+2).\n\\]\nExpanding gives\n\\[\n12x^2+2x+4=12x^2+4x+4.\n\\]\nThe quadratic terms and constants match on both sides, so subtracting them leaves \\(2x=4x\\). Subtracting \\(2x\\) gives \\(0=2x\\), hence \\(x=0\\).\n\nFor \\(x=0\\), the condition \\(y=2x\\) gives \\(y=0\\), and both sides of the original equation equal \\(4\\). Since the preceding steps are reversible, this is the only solution. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
